# online_softmax formal: BMC over the module's own `ifdef FORMAL block
# (rtl/online_softmax.sv, docs/uarch.md 8.2). Scope is deliberately narrow
# per CLAUDE.md ("formal is a subset / do not fight the tool"): the
# transition relation still contains the 1024-entry ROM function and the
# 24u x 16u multiply (both are part of the combinational next-state logic
# feeding the asserted registers), so the solver reasons about them even
# though no property directly asserts ROM contents or product values. What
# IS proven here, every BMC step up to depth 20:
#   - idx() clamp facts: diff_m, diff_s <= 0; pre-clamp and final LUT
#     indices stay in their declared ranges (combinational, docs 3.5/5).
#   - reset state: m == -32768, l == 0, out_valid == 0.
#   - hold (in_valid low the prior cycle): (m, l, w, r) unchanged,
#     out_valid low (no stale valid pulse).
#   - accepted element: out_valid rises exactly one cycle later; on a
#     row_start step m becomes exactly the incoming s (identity 2, uarch
#     6.1); otherwise m is monotonic non-decreasing within a row (the
#     running-max-monotonic invariant).
# Rich behavioral checks (LUT entry-for-entry equivalence, full recurrence
# vs the model/attn.py mirror, DEN accumulator overflow bound over long
# random rows) are cocotb's job (tb/online_softmax/test_online_softmax.py),
# not formal's: an open bmc flow proving a 40-bit multiply/1024-way ROM
# functionally correct is exactly the kind of proof this toolchain cannot
# do in reasonable time, so those checks are deliberately NOT attempted
# here (CLAUDE.md formal-limit note).
#
# Depth settled at 20 (same as mac_unit.sby): passes in a few seconds with
# z3, well inside the ~3 minute budget, so no need to step down to 10/5.
[options]
mode bmc
depth 20

[engines]
smtbmc z3

[script]
read_verilog -sv -formal -DFORMAL online_softmax.sv
prep -top online_softmax

[files]
rtl/online_softmax.sv
rtl/exp_lut.svh rtl/exp_lut.svh
